WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.830
Welcome to Ear Biscuits.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:05.663
I'm Rhett.

00:00:05.663 --> 00:00:06.930
And I'm Link.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.200
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.200
we ask what's it like inside NASA.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:16.573
Yes.

00:00:16.573 --> 00:00:17.620
And we should know.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:20.970
Because we've been inside NASA.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.700
Houston we have a problem.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.914
We've let Rhett and Link inside.

00:00:24.914 --> 00:00:27.973
Yeah, you know the saying
Houston we have a problem?

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.510
Well it's because if you're in
space and you have a problem

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:35.040
you tell Houston, and
that's where we were,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.490
in Houston at the Space Center there.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.810
The Johnson Space Center.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.001
Yeah.

00:00:41.001 --> 00:00:45.463
They graciously invited
us and gave us a tour.

00:00:47.603 --> 00:00:49.860
I learned some stuff.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:50.860
Yeah.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.360
I feel like the wheels are
really turning for you.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.670
So I really wanna know what came of that

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:56.960
cause we never discussed it,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.110
cause we had to do a show that night.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:00:59.943
Right.

00:00:59.943 --> 00:01:02.750
So we'll dedicate this episode

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.133
to unpacking NASA's secrets.

00:01:07.581 --> 00:01:10.540
The secrets that they're
willing to disclose

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.600
to anyone from the
public who comes inside.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.990
But we are fresh off
of a little mini tour,

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:21.404
we were in Houston for our comedy show.

00:01:21.404 --> 00:01:23.160
And we went to, shout out to Houston,

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:28.070
New Orleans, Birmingham,
Jacksonville, and Tampa.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:32.540
We had some fun on all those stages.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.970
All those stages.
All those stages.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.080
It's like a stage five rocket.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.870
Yeah, and you know what, we're not done

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:40.971
with the fun while we're
talking about that.

00:01:40.971 --> 00:01:41.870
Okay, yeah.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.740
We've actually got, again,

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.000
this is just, we're not
planning on touring much

00:01:47.838 --> 00:01:49.070
in the near future after this,

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:52.160
take a pretty good break from that.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.140
So this is the last chance
to see this particular show

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:57.810
for a good while.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.690
Yeah, just this fall of 2019,

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.630
just go to RhettandLinklive.com,

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:07.470
but to wet your appetite,
first of all we've got a week

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:12.470
of special, well I'm just
gonna call it a special thing

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.083
called--

00:02:15.083 --> 00:02:16.110
The Bleak Creek Conversations.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.700
Bleak Creek Conversations
where around the launch

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.530
of the novel, we're planning
a very, very special

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:26.530
evening in Boston, New York,
Chicago, Dallas-Fort Worth,

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.560
two shows in Winston Salem, North Carolina

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.030
and then back here in Los Angeles,

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:34.370
and that goes from October
27th through November 1st,

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:35.800
November 3rd, I'm sorry.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.430
And then we're doing more
of our musical comedy show

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.312
in Albuquerque, Phoenix, Sacramento,

00:02:41.312 --> 00:02:44.410
and Valley Center, California
November 20th through 23rd.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:47.070
So go to RhettandLinklive.com
to get tickets.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:47.903
But um,

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.380
we did have some fun.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.590
We didn't bring the kids this time.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:54.710
Briton was still there.

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.410
Yeah he was, he's a kid, he's 19.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:57.243
Yeah, well.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:00.160
Technically he isn't.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.160
Oh and let me, I do wanna clarify

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.580
cause I wanna say it,
every time we talk about

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.200
the Bleak Creek
Conversations that you a copy

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:09.810
of the novel when you get that ticket.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.320
So think about that.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:12.153
It's a deal.

00:03:12.153 --> 00:03:14.540
As you're considering coming out to that.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.250
And we're showing a
documentary that we shot

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.590
back home in Buies Creek,
North Carolina over the summer.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:23.820
So, it's gonna be an intimate,
one of a kind evening.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:25.370
And then for these shows, I mean,

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:27.400
I really think we're stretching our legs.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:32.400
Like, we had some crazy moments with fans.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:36.810
I wanna give a shout out
to you guys for being

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.570
so supportive and letting us talk about

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:40.590
whatever we wanna talk about.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:42.010
We don't even know what's gonna happen.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.820
We're talking about like soil eating fish.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.280
You remember soil eating fish?

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:47.290
That was one night.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:48.123
Right.

00:03:48.973 --> 00:03:50.103
We talked extensively about that.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:52.150
Another night we talked,

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.330
and I can't even explain
it, you had to be there.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.096
Well that's the thing is that the,

00:03:57.096 --> 00:03:58.980
each show ends up being different,

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.710
we have some things
that we say before songs

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:06.000
that we always say, but
even that changes up.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.000
But we use the energy and the vibe

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.440
of that particular location.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.591
And sometimes it's just in a weird place.

00:04:15.591 --> 00:04:17.400
We channel the voodoo of New Orleans.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.233
We saw some voodoo dolls.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.470
We gotta tell this story
that we told from stage.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:25.270
We were in this place cause you and Briton

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:27.501
are into all this type of stuff,

00:04:27.501 --> 00:04:29.270
and it turns out voodoo dolls
are supposed to be nice,

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.650
mostly used for positive purposes.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:35.810
Well voodoo as a whole,
voodoo as a whole, which,

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:37.690
okay, I'm probably gonna
get some things wrong,

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.360
so lemme give that disclaimer.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.370
You're not an expert.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:41.203
I'm not an expert.

00:04:41.203 --> 00:04:42.036
You shouldn't be.

00:04:42.036 --> 00:04:44.230
But I've been to the voodoo
museum in New Orleans

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:46.580
and that has given me
enough of an authority

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:51.580
to say that voodoo is like a meeting of,

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:56.070
you know, predominantly
African ancestral religions

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:00.160
meet some elements of Western
religion like Catholicism,

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.080
and kind of, so sort of a
hybrid sort of comes out

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.150
and that's what you ended
up having in New Orleans,

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:08.170
in the New Orleans area.

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:11.260
But I think that culturally

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:13.370
we tend to be kind of fascinated with

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:16.193
what we see as like the subversive,

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:21.000
maybe we would say evil side of voodoo

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.460
cause it's like witchcraft and spells

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:26.244
and sacrificing chickens
and stuff like that.

00:05:26.244 --> 00:05:28.200
Poking pins in a doll so
that you can hurt somebody.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.780
But in reality, most of that stuff,

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:33.970
the intention of it is all good.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.010
Like you said, to bring
good to someone, but yeah,

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:38.330
sometimes you might want to
bring some bad to somebody.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:40.180
But I'll tell you what you can't do is

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.290
you can't roll into one
of these voodoo shops

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.750
and take a picture of the voodoo dolls.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.830
They won't let you do that.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.010
So we're walking around,

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:50.000
it says no photos, no photos.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.800
And I heard a girl come up to Rhett,

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.853
and it turned out to be two girls,

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.270
and I was a little further away but I did

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:01.313
hear the entire conversation.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:06.480
And, but do you wanna tell
it since she said it to you?

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.830
Yeah, well, for those of you who haven't

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:10.480
been to New Orleans before,

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.190
it doesn't matter what
time of the year it is,

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:13.840
a lot of times there are just people.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:15.040
Or time of day.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:15.873
Everyone is walking around.

00:06:17.114 --> 00:06:17.947
Yeah, cause this was like one o'clock.

00:06:17.947 --> 00:06:22.860
Everyone is walking around
with large beverage containers

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:26.640
of different sizes and
shapes, often very tall.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.380
Like the one that this girl had was.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:29.213
Big as her head.

00:06:29.213 --> 00:06:31.300
36 inches tall.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:33.214
This giant, plastic.

00:06:33.214 --> 00:06:34.829
She had a 36 inch doll head.

00:06:34.829 --> 00:06:38.240
Plastic cup full of alcohol.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:39.073
Well who knows what.

00:06:39.073 --> 00:06:39.906
A lot of times it's just--

00:06:39.906 --> 00:06:41.414
Voodoo doll piss, who knows?

00:06:41.414 --> 00:06:42.247
A slushie.

00:06:42.247 --> 00:06:44.570
I mean, they got whole
bars that behind the bar

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:48.940
instead of seeing taps it's
just alcoholic slushies.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:50.732
Yeah, for the children.

00:06:50.732 --> 00:06:54.930
And, so she comes up and
she's already a little bit

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:56.123
New Orleans-ed,

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:01.043
and she sees me and she says oh,

00:07:02.668 --> 00:07:04.010
and then she sees Link kind of behind me

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:07.623
and she's like oh aren't
you guys Thomas and Rhett?

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:13.590
And I joined the
conversation at this point

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.170
in order to say--

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:16.003
Why yes.

00:07:16.003 --> 00:07:17.810
Well yes we are.

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:20.890
Well, the interesting thing
is we've gotten mistaken.

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:22.010
Thomas and Rhett.

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:23.280
We've been called lots of things.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.780
Thomas Rhett is a country music performer,

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.280
if you do not know that.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:30.220
So that's what makes
this particularly funny

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:32.550
because she was getting
in her mind the only

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.040
Rhett that she knew was Thomas Rhett

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:35.833
so aren't you Thomas and Rhett.

00:07:36.933 --> 00:07:39.320
That's the best one we've ever gotten.

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.730
Yeah, and then she took a picture

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:44.430
and then the guy who worked
there is like no pictures.

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:48.450
And we're like well she's
taking a picture of us,

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.367
Thomas and Rhett, she's
not taking a picture--

00:07:51.367 --> 00:07:52.200
Don't you know who we are?

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.110
Of your voodoo dolls.

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:55.060
She certainly does.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:58.680
They had a glass case with
a paper mache head in it,

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:01.640
and then they had a news paper clipping

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:03.580
that's like this head
had been found somewhere

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:05.140
but it was clearly paper mache.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:05.973
Yeah, right.

00:08:05.973 --> 00:08:07.357
I don't know if that was a recreation.

00:08:07.357 --> 00:08:10.220
I think it's just all part of the act.

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:11.610
So we did that.

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:12.890
Oh and we ate.

00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:15.830
This is also New Orleans,
where we ate lunch,

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:19.080
I had a spiritual experience.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:23.470
I mean, you wait in line, and
then you order at the counter

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:26.940
and they're like frying chicken
and frying fish and shrimp,

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:29.850
and chicken was the special,

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:32.820
everyone was getting like,
it was like a meat and three

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:34.457
type of situation, you get your meat

00:08:34.457 --> 00:08:37.000
and your three sides, three veggies.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.471
But I think it was two.

00:08:38.471 --> 00:08:40.243
It was meat and two, okay.

00:08:40.243 --> 00:08:44.090
And, you know, I'm like, well
I kind of want some shrimp,

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:45.740
I like a good fried shrimp
at a place like this,

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:48.270
so I ordered that, but all you
guys got the fried chicken.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:49.910
It did look very good,
but I didn't get it.

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:51.300
This place was called,

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.550
I wanted to say Margerie's but it wasn't,

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.360
it was Majoria's, with no R,

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:58.700
Majoria's Commerce Restaurant.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:00.040
Lot of locals in there.

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:02.470
And when you're up there
getting ready to order

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:06.350
it seems like chaos and
no matter what we did

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:08.714
it seems like we were always
doing the wrong thing.

00:09:08.714 --> 00:09:09.547
Like you're not supposed to,

00:09:09.547 --> 00:09:11.800
like she would yell at
us like come over here.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:13.870
Say, I got you, well have you ordered?

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:15.629
It's like well, no,

00:09:15.629 --> 00:09:17.480
can you order, what do you want?

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:19.487
It was like the taking of the orders

00:09:19.487 --> 00:09:21.970
like there was an aggressive.

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:23.109
Frustration.

00:09:23.109 --> 00:09:24.248
Sort of stance.

00:09:24.248 --> 00:09:25.180
And I was like, you know
what, this feels right,

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:28.030
you know, if you're going
into a place where you see,

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:30.650
I mean I saw a guy who
looked like he was on meth,

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:32.890
I saw a good who looked
like he could be the mayor

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:36.120
of New Orleans, they were
both there eating at tables,

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:37.710
eating the same fried chicken.

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:39.380
What if the mayor of
New Orleans is on meth?

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:40.980
Well let's assume not.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:42.025
Okay.

00:09:42.025 --> 00:09:45.490
I'm just saying there was
like a politician dude,

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:48.970
and you know, it was a
melting pot of locals.

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:51.160
And then we just felt constant shame

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:52.020
for doing everything wrong.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:53.450
I'm like this is how you should feel.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:55.620
You know you're in the right place

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:57.870
when you feel like you're
screwing it all up.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:00.570
Well when Briton sat down
after being through that line

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:02.020
he was like whoa, that was a very

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:04.720
stress inducing experience.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:05.553
Yeah.

00:10:05.553 --> 00:10:08.010
And it was, cause you just
felt like an outsider.

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:10.440
But then it was replaced with the--

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:11.273
Pure joy.

00:10:11.273 --> 00:10:12.570
Dining experience.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.203
Shrimp were amazing.

00:10:14.203 --> 00:10:16.340
They had these twice baked,

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:17.850
they called them twice baked potatoes,

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:20.010
but they scooped it out
just like mashed potatoes

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:22.390
and put gravy on it still, it was amazing.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:23.230
Right.

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:24.240
No skins.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.450
There was no skins on
the twice baked potatoes.

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:27.283
And corn!

00:10:27.283 --> 00:10:28.770
And I'm going through this shrimp

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:29.890
like there's no tomorrow.

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:30.723
Loving it.

00:10:30.723 --> 00:10:32.270
You guys are saying the chicken's amazing.

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:34.860
I look over and I see
half a chicken leg uneaten

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:37.080
on Briton's plate and I'm like we're blood

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:39.330
you done with that chicken leg?

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:41.260
Are you gonna eat that?

00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:42.093
He's like you can have it,

00:10:42.093 --> 00:10:44.115
I took a bite of that chicken leg.

00:10:44.115 --> 00:10:44.980
It's the best chicken.

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:49.980
I was transported to my
nanny's Sunday after church

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:55.030
kitchen table where she
would always fry up chicken.

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:58.723
She, oh man, I got goosebumps,

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:04.130
and then I started talking
about how good this chicken was,

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:09.130
and I got my goosebumps,
I got goosebump bumped,

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:14.060
and it just felt so good
to eat that chicken.

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:14.893
It was the best.

00:11:14.893 --> 00:11:15.726
I was eating Nanny's chicken again.

00:11:15.726 --> 00:11:18.600
It was the best fried chicken.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:19.660
Oh I've ever had.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:21.132
I mean, I can safely say.

00:11:21.132 --> 00:11:22.310
I've had a lot of fried chicken.

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:24.370
I haven't had friend chicken than that.

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:25.890
Maybe there's a time that I've forgotten

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:27.650
that I had as good of fried chicken,

00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:31.481
but the ratio of crispiness on the outside

00:11:31.481 --> 00:11:33.810
to juiciness on the inside,

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:35.560
and of course I'm a white meat man,

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:37.310
and a lot of people are
turned off by white meat

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:38.740
because they think that it's dry

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:40.230
and they think that a breast is dry.

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:41.819
Yeah.

00:11:41.819 --> 00:11:42.652
But I think that the breast is the perfect

00:11:42.652 --> 00:11:44.970
piece of chicken because
it's the most chicken

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:48.270
concentrated without
any hindrance of bone.

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:50.518
It's just, you have a
giant piece of muscle.

00:11:50.518 --> 00:11:52.260
But you don't get as much of that crunchy

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:54.340
and that skin, I like that too.

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:57.527
You get a huge blanket of skin, man.

00:11:57.527 --> 00:11:59.886
You gotta, you gotta ration that out.

00:11:59.886 --> 00:12:02.840
The largest concentration of skin and meat

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:03.750
on the bird.

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:05.710
It's like the place that
you're intended to take

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:06.730
a bite out of a bird.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:08.170
If you were just to pick up a bird,

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:09.210
you would just take a bite out that area.

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:11.742
I'm a dark meat man, but, I mean, listen.

00:12:11.742 --> 00:12:12.890
But hold on, but it was so juicy.

00:12:12.890 --> 00:12:13.910
It didn't matter.

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:17.920
That it would turn a dark meat
man into a white meat man.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:19.010
Now I didn't even let you have any

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:20.610
cause I wasn't gonna give any up.

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:22.040
But Briton did, thank goodness,

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:25.220
and it was absolutely transformative.

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:26.720
I mean, it was transportative.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.364
It transported me.

00:12:29.364 --> 00:12:32.700
Cause my nanny's not able
to fry chicken anymore

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:36.210
and she had, you know, there's
something to the brineing,

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:39.030
like if it's really salty
you're on to something.

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:40.707
You gotta get the brineing right.

00:12:40.707 --> 00:12:42.444
The brineing does something with it.

00:12:42.444 --> 00:12:43.277
Something with milk.

00:12:43.277 --> 00:12:44.110
The juiciness though.

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:46.941
Did something with brine
and milk or something.

00:12:46.941 --> 00:12:51.941
But it's apparently irreplicable
until this place Majoria's.

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:55.850
Highly recommend it if
you're in New Orleans.

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:58.420
Oh yeah, it was just, it was good, I mean.

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:00.810
Now I will say, cause speaking of food,

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:04.230
we were doing the Chow
Down in Your Town thing

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:06.450
that we do before each show where we take

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:09.840
the two things that are
recommended to us on Twitter

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:12.070
and we pit them against each other.

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:16.460
And, I can't even remember
what the New Orleans'

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:18.150
recommendations were, that's how.

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:19.480
It was gu--

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:20.600
Oh it was gumbo.
Gumbo.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:22.140
And gumbo and beignets.

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:24.400
And it was beignets Cafe du Monde,

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:25.870
which is the place to get beignets,

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:29.210
but they had been in
the bag for 45 minutes

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:30.220
by the time they got to us

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:31.830
and you really need to eat those fresh.

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:32.973
They still won.

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:35.810
We thought the gumbo was,

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:38.033
should have been jambalaya

00:13:38.033 --> 00:13:39.850
and we were trying to figure
out why it wasn't red.

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:41.120
But it was a pot of gravy.

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:44.240
We were just super ignorant
about what jambalaya is

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:47.300
versus gumbo, and gumbo,
because of the rue,

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:48.380
is supposed to be brown.

00:13:48.380 --> 00:13:50.570
But I just don't, I mean it wasn't bad,

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:52.850
I ate an entire, like,
you know, cup of it.

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:55.850
You eating an entirety of
something says nothing about it.

00:13:55.850 --> 00:13:57.330
Right, that's what I'm saying,

00:13:57.330 --> 00:13:59.000
it was edible, but it wasn't like

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:00.380
I have to tell someone about this

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:02.150
so they will also eat it,

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:04.810
which is what I would do
with the fried chicken.

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:08.010
I don't lick the plate
unless it's lickable.

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:09.520
You lick the plate because.

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:10.353
I'm hungry.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:12.123
I'm always hungry.

00:14:12.123 --> 00:14:12.956
It's edible.

00:14:12.956 --> 00:14:14.450
I'm hungry right now and I just ate.

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:19.240
And then we went to an escape room.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:20.960
You know it's like--

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:21.978
In Tampa.

00:14:21.978 --> 00:14:26.050
Am I wrong to say that escape rooms are

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:28.140
not like a hot bed of coolness.

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:30.307
It's like you just
don't think you'd like--

00:14:30.307 --> 00:14:32.170
But what is a hot bed of coolness?

00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:33.800
Like.
Give me an example.

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:36.960
Like a rock and roll bar,
like out back in the ally,

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:38.640
like if you're walking in the back ally

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:42.190
and you see like, I don't know, you see,

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:43.510
somebody from Motley Crew back there.

00:14:43.510 --> 00:14:45.420
Maybe in movies.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:48.660
Escape rooms are kind
of like, I don't know.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:50.630
For nerds, is that what you're saying?

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:52.263
Yeah, it's kind of, well, and then it's,

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:57.430
it's like you've got this grand idea,

00:14:57.430 --> 00:14:58.550
we're gonna put people in a room,

00:14:58.550 --> 00:14:59.840
they're gonna solve these problems

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:02.370
but it's gonna be immersive

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:05.090
and you're gonna forget where you are

00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:07.120
and it's gonna be like Disney Land.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:10.540
But you never quite forget
that you're in a strip mall.

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:11.580
Right.

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:12.413
It's hard.

00:15:12.413 --> 00:15:13.938
In an old converted office building.

00:15:13.938 --> 00:15:16.880
Yeah, it's hard to make the set dressing

00:15:18.030 --> 00:15:21.680
work that well in what
used to be a dental office.

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:23.760
Right, but we had a great time.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:26.310
No, escaping a dental office,

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:27.500
hey, maybe that's a win, win,

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:29.070
maybe that's what we should do.

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:33.100
Just, acquire a dental
office and just lock it.

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:36.710
Put some clues in there,
you gotta pull some teeth

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:38.350
to get some keys.

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:39.490
I could get with that.

00:15:39.490 --> 00:15:42.824
The one in Tampa was, it
was pretty fun though,

00:15:42.824 --> 00:15:45.290
it was like, it was zombie apocalypse,

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:47.620
and I'm like I don't wanna
do this if there's like

00:15:47.620 --> 00:15:49.200
people dressed as zombies, I just,

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:51.090
I'm not, I don't wanna have to have

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:52.360
that type of interaction.

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:53.570
But as soon as you went in the place

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:54.900
you kind of knew there wasn't gonna be

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:57.030
anybody dressed as anything.

00:15:57.030 --> 00:16:00.700
It's just gonna be some
faint sound effects

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:05.001
of zombies outside, and
we are inside tasked with

00:16:05.001 --> 00:16:08.990
developing the, unlocking the antidote

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:10.960
for the zombie apocalypse.

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:13.060
Pretty cool actually, we had a lot of fun.

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:13.893
Yeah we did.

00:16:13.893 --> 00:16:16.100
We didn't get in, we
didn't fight too much.

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:16.933
And we got out.

00:16:16.933 --> 00:16:17.940
You and I didn't fight any,

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:19.540
I think Briton and I fought.

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:21.410
Which I think is, that's the great,

00:16:21.410 --> 00:16:23.540
I think that's the dynamic
when Briton's there

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:27.320
it's like is that what happens?

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:29.890
Like, you know, I start to get grumpy

00:16:29.890 --> 00:16:31.300
after a couple of days on the road.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:32.475
True.

00:16:32.475 --> 00:16:33.430
And I'm like, I think he's there to,

00:16:33.430 --> 00:16:35.150
he's there to absorb that.

00:16:35.150 --> 00:16:36.730
He absorbs your grumpiness?

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:38.530
I direct it towards him sometimes.

00:16:39.481 --> 00:16:42.030
Do you observe my?

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:43.070
Absorption?

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:44.073
His absorption?

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:47.850
Yeah, I mean I actually think that

00:16:48.818 --> 00:16:53.230
he changes the dynamic of
us spending that much time

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:54.790
together on the road.

00:16:54.790 --> 00:16:55.623
Yeah.

00:16:55.623 --> 00:16:58.260
Because I think that, we talked about this

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:02.150
on this past trip, which
apparently I lost my voice

00:17:02.150 --> 00:17:04.150
on the trip because.

00:17:04.150 --> 00:17:06.208
Do you need to hack something up?

00:17:06.208 --> 00:17:07.880
Yeah, I've got like a--

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:10.150
Do you want me to cut the
throat of your voodoo doll,

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:11.600
give you a little tracheotomy?

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:16.600
I've got like a constant
production of phlegm,

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:20.550
that's just like, it hasn't
been happening until right now.

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:22.370
Well it started happening
with me yesterday

00:17:22.370 --> 00:17:25.380
and I think it's a return to Los Angeles.

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:26.800
There's something in the air here.

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:27.633
The pollution?

00:17:27.633 --> 00:17:30.439
Yeah, something, I'm serious.

00:17:30.439 --> 00:17:31.520
Well I know there's a lot of pollution.

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:33.390
My nose started running a little bit more.

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:36.600
My throat felt a little congested.

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:37.690
Well I guess I'm gonna be struggling

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:38.523
with it the whole time.

00:17:38.523 --> 00:17:39.863
We should move to Arizona.

00:17:41.051 --> 00:17:42.557
I don't even know what I was gonna say.

00:17:42.557 --> 00:17:43.550
Oh, he changes the dynamic,

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:46.983
because when we spend a lot
of time together on the road,

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:51.230
first of all, our work
responsibilities back here

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:53.350
don't go away, now we've got a great team

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:57.410
and there's not a whole
lot on a day to day basis

00:17:57.410 --> 00:17:58.793
that we have to worry about anymore

00:17:58.793 --> 00:18:00.090
form the company's standpoint.

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:01.500
But there's still a lot of things,

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:03.430
and then Jenna is there,
she's getting a lot of emails,

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:04.480
she's handling a lot of things,

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:05.930
asking us a lot of questions.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:10.170
We made the observation
that we don't get a lot done

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:17.140
other than touring even though the act of

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:20.100
getting ready for the show,
you know signing the posters

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:22.330
for the meet and greet
and then meeting some,

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:24.280
you know, meeting some of
the Mythical Society people,

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:25.820
or anybody else that might be in that town

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:26.850
that we've arranged to meet,

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:29.430
and then you do the show,

00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:30.900
and then you've got the
meet and greet after.

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:34.463
I mean that's all the
work, work that we do.

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:39.310
But it's pretty emotionally draining.

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:41.320
And so if you're gonna do something else

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:42.960
you want it to be like
something that can kind of

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:47.268
be engaging like going and
eating fried chicken, or,

00:18:47.268 --> 00:18:49.880
going to an escape room.
Going to an escape room.

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:51.840
We did get out by the way.

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:53.830
Yeah, which apparently only 35% of people

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:56.630
who go into this escape room
get out and I gotta say--

00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:59.230
That's what she told us to make us

00:18:59.230 --> 00:19:00.063
feel good about getting out.

00:19:00.063 --> 00:19:03.110
Do you think they're lying
about the escape percentage?

00:19:03.110 --> 00:19:04.940
That makes absolutely, there's no,

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:08.470
I can think of zero reason
why they would lie about that.

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:10.290
I just think people,
if people don't escape

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:11.410
aren't they angry.

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:12.570
I've been to an escape room before

00:19:12.570 --> 00:19:13.430
and not gotten out?

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:15.830
And aren't they still in there?

00:19:15.830 --> 00:19:17.409
When the time's up they just let you out

00:19:17.409 --> 00:19:18.470
and say you're a failure?

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:20.150
Yeah, escape rooms.

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:21.940
You didn't escape from an escape room?

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:23.570
I've only escaped from escape rooms.

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:26.030
Would not be fun for
people for people who like

00:19:26.030 --> 00:19:28.040
escape rooms if people who were coming to

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:31.090
an escape room for the
first time always get out.

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:33.660
That seems pretty straight forward to me.

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:34.697
Because.
Yeah, yeah.

00:19:34.697 --> 00:19:38.840
And we had very motivated,
we were all very motivated,

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:40.440
and we all worked well together.

00:19:41.530 --> 00:19:42.680
And then Briton and his friends

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:45.200
had been to an escape
room like the day before

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:46.610
so they were kind of primed.

00:19:46.610 --> 00:19:51.580
There's only so many different
ways you can hide a key

00:19:52.730 --> 00:19:56.150
in a box, you know what I'm saying?

00:19:56.150 --> 00:19:57.890
They had some pretty cool ones.

00:19:57.890 --> 00:20:00.825
It was very cool, but,

00:20:00.825 --> 00:20:03.664
or it wasn't cool cause you
don't think it can be cool.

00:20:03.664 --> 00:20:04.497
It's not cool.

00:20:04.497 --> 00:20:08.290
It's like magic without a magician,

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:10.261
is kind of what it feels like.

00:20:10.261 --> 00:20:13.100
It feels like you're doing all the magic,

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:14.680
but it's just puzzles.

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:15.950
That's an interesting perspective.

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:18.600
I don't know, it feels like
it's in the same genre.

00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:21.790
Even though there's no magic involved.

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:23.750
You think people who like magic

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:25.010
also like escape rooms?

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:27.654
Yeah, big overlap there.

00:20:27.654 --> 00:20:29.460
That makes sense to me.

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:34.240
I can't exactly explain
why, but I agree with it.

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:37.123
But we got out, so we are
in the top 35 of all people.

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:40.800
Top 35%.
Top 35 people.

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:43.330
We're the top 35 people on the planet

00:20:43.330 --> 00:20:46.890
cause we escaped from an
escape room in Tampa, Florida.

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:49.800
Yeah we're in the top 35%
of people who have escaped

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:52.650
a very particular escape room in Tampa.

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:54.840
And I gotta be honest with
you, nothing against Tampa,

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:57.210
or nothing against any place in the world,

00:20:57.210 --> 00:20:59.270
but when I found out that 35% of people

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:01.320
had made it out of this
escape room in Tampa

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.500
I was like I'm gonna feel
pretty bad about myself

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:06.720
if I don't qualify in the top 35%.

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:07.553
Mm.

00:21:07.553 --> 00:21:09.415
I mean that's just kind of pride,

00:21:09.415 --> 00:21:10.357
that's how much pride I have.

00:21:10.357 --> 00:21:13.889
And I was like especially with
the team that we've got here.

00:21:13.889 --> 00:21:14.722
I feel like this is gonna happen,

00:21:14.722 --> 00:21:16.170
we don't have any strangers,

00:21:16.170 --> 00:21:18.750
no randos, sometimes you
get randos in your group.

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:19.583
That's bad.

00:21:19.583 --> 00:21:20.760
I had a drunk rando.

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:22.940
The only time I've never gotten out of,

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:23.773
I didn't get out of escape room,

00:21:23.773 --> 00:21:27.050
is because there was a very
distracting drunk rando

00:21:27.050 --> 00:21:28.303
in the room with us.

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:30.133
Hated that guy.

00:21:30.133 --> 00:21:32.190
At a certain point, I
remember you told me,

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:34.570
you just kind of have to
turn to him and say dude.

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:36.522
He couldn't take any--
Sit in a corner.

00:21:36.522 --> 00:21:37.355
He could take no direction.

00:21:37.355 --> 00:21:38.910
Sit in a corner man.
He was such a distraction.

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:40.010
Sit in a corner and make sure you're not

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:42.870
sitting on anything like
a key, a lock, or a clue.

00:21:42.870 --> 00:21:43.703
Right.

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:46.363
So we went to NASA.

00:21:47.750 --> 00:21:51.070
More questions than answers
perhaps, let's get to those.

00:21:51.070 --> 00:21:55.300
But first, let's ask you to buy stuff.

