00:45:25.020 --> 00:45:26.840
Somebody sprayed all over the floor,

00:45:26.840 --> 00:45:31.840
Briton, Lando, dad, which one of you guys

00:45:32.320 --> 00:45:33.801
splattered all over the floor?

00:45:33.801 --> 00:45:35.218
I think it's Briton or Lando.

00:45:35.218 --> 00:45:36.051
Thank you.

00:45:36.051 --> 00:45:37.060
And if I had to narrow it down to one

00:45:37.060 --> 00:45:38.360
I would say it was Briton.

00:45:40.030 --> 00:45:42.700
We all looked at him and then he said

00:45:42.700 --> 00:45:45.180
I haven't used the bathroom
since I've gotten back.

00:45:45.180 --> 00:45:46.893
I was like it's been four hours.

00:45:47.760 --> 00:45:51.040
You've been back four
hours homie, you're lying.

00:45:51.040 --> 00:45:53.740
No, I went in the bathroom
and we put in this,

00:45:53.740 --> 00:45:56.350
we put in black tile on the ground.

00:45:56.350 --> 00:45:59.183
Here's a hot tip, don't put black, like,

00:45:59.183 --> 00:46:04.183
matte finished tile in a boys bathroom

00:46:04.870 --> 00:46:08.840
because you can see, with
the way the lighting is.

00:46:08.840 --> 00:46:10.620
You can see all the splatter all the time.

00:46:10.620 --> 00:46:12.380
Splatter everywhere.

00:46:12.380 --> 00:46:14.590
It's like looking at a
crime scene where the serial

00:46:14.590 --> 00:46:15.900
killer used only urine.

00:46:15.900 --> 00:46:16.733
Right.

00:46:17.713 --> 00:46:19.690
I think there was a serial killer,

00:46:19.690 --> 00:46:21.337
they called him the Pee Pee Man.

00:46:21.337 --> 00:46:23.130
Pee Pee Killer.

00:46:23.130 --> 00:46:24.393
The Pee Pee Sprayer.

00:46:25.940 --> 00:46:28.780
Lincolns like, first of
all I was proud of him

00:46:28.780 --> 00:46:30.070
that he was outraged about it.

00:46:30.070 --> 00:46:32.190
Even though I still feel
like maybe he did it.

00:46:32.190 --> 00:46:35.550
They called him the Golden Shower Killer.

00:46:35.550 --> 00:46:36.663
Not the Golden State.

00:46:36.663 --> 00:46:39.330
With the Golden State,
what was that guy's name?

00:46:39.330 --> 00:46:42.920
Golden State, Chris Moane, Chris Mullens?

00:46:42.920 --> 00:46:44.370
I was trying to come up with
a pun and I can't do it.

00:46:44.370 --> 00:46:46.959
I feel like the phlegm is construction,

00:46:46.959 --> 00:46:50.112
is constricting my ability to make puns.

00:46:50.112 --> 00:46:51.271
Obstructing.

00:46:51.271 --> 00:46:53.070
Obstructing, I can't even
come up with the right words.

00:46:53.070 --> 00:46:55.263
You are not astronaut material.

00:46:56.680 --> 00:47:01.310
So, I ended up making Lando
clean it up with a rag.

00:47:01.310 --> 00:47:05.210
But I'm just saying,
hot tip, no black floors

00:47:05.210 --> 00:47:06.227
in a boys room.

00:47:06.227 --> 00:47:09.230
And I was like guys, I'm just telling you,

00:47:09.230 --> 00:47:13.850
you just need to sit down
to pee like the astronauts

00:47:13.850 --> 00:47:18.850
and me because you never
know, it's very unpredictable,

00:47:20.040 --> 00:47:22.150
it's a lot of distance between.

00:47:22.150 --> 00:47:23.623
John Wayne Spraycy.

00:47:24.740 --> 00:47:27.195
There it is, you got it.

00:47:27.195 --> 00:47:29.760
I knew I'd find it somewhere.

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:31.250
Okay, you found it.

00:47:31.250 --> 00:47:33.270
That was pretty good.

00:47:33.270 --> 00:47:34.560
John Wayne Spraycy.

00:47:35.900 --> 00:47:38.250
Okay, so back to astronauts.

00:47:38.250 --> 00:47:40.430
So we think that they
should be donating sperm,

00:47:40.430 --> 00:47:41.790
not to build a master race,

00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:43.890
just to supplement sperm banks,

00:47:43.890 --> 00:47:47.010
just to make people a little bit smarter.

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:50.670
I just don't think they're
big on personality.

00:47:50.670 --> 00:47:52.620
I'm not saying that I didn't like him,

00:47:52.620 --> 00:47:54.290
I didn't like the astronauts that I met.

00:47:54.290 --> 00:47:55.610
Well they're not very reactive.

00:47:55.610 --> 00:47:57.810
Yeah they're very, they're so even keeled.

00:47:57.810 --> 00:47:59.040
You can't be reactive, you have to be

00:47:59.040 --> 00:48:00.180
a non-reactionary person.

00:48:00.180 --> 00:48:04.260
I just don't think that makes
for a fun space situation.

00:48:04.260 --> 00:48:06.150
It's like when everybody's unflappable.

00:48:06.150 --> 00:48:07.640
You don't want, you're saying you want it

00:48:07.640 --> 00:48:09.680
to be like Bachelor in Paradise up there?

00:48:09.680 --> 00:48:10.790
You don't want that.

00:48:10.790 --> 00:48:14.886
You keep bringing it back to
like spreading seed and stuff

00:48:14.886 --> 00:48:16.860
I'm just trying to make it about fun.

00:48:16.860 --> 00:48:19.984
First of all, this is a clear indication--

00:48:19.984 --> 00:48:20.910
You don't have to have sex to have fun.

00:48:20.910 --> 00:48:22.530
This is a clear indication
that you thought

00:48:22.530 --> 00:48:24.190
that my reference to Bachelor in Paradise

00:48:24.190 --> 00:48:26.326
had to do with sex.

00:48:26.326 --> 00:48:27.159
Yeah.

00:48:27.159 --> 00:48:29.600
Jacob and Kiko clearly knew I just meant

00:48:29.600 --> 00:48:31.300
putting emotionally unstable people

00:48:31.300 --> 00:48:32.950
together in a small space.

00:48:32.950 --> 00:48:33.783
Oh.

00:48:33.783 --> 00:48:34.980
That's why they both left.

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:37.090
Because reality television
is about putting

00:48:37.090 --> 00:48:41.130
emotionally unstable people
together in a confined space

00:48:41.130 --> 00:48:42.900
and that doesn't have
to be like a confined

00:48:42.900 --> 00:48:45.370
physical space, but it's
just like confining them

00:48:45.370 --> 00:48:48.620
in some way, like you can
only talk to these people.

00:48:48.620 --> 00:48:50.450
So if you put those
kind of people together

00:48:50.450 --> 00:48:51.860
in the International Space Station.

00:48:51.860 --> 00:48:53.350
Jacob says with alcohol.

00:48:53.350 --> 00:48:54.636
With alcohol, right.

00:48:54.636 --> 00:48:58.770
The whole space station would just somehow

00:48:59.960 --> 00:49:02.840
end up getting burned
up in the atmosphere.

00:49:02.840 --> 00:49:05.120
It just wouldn't, it couldn't succeed.

00:49:05.120 --> 00:49:07.590
Now, but if we could all
watch it on television

00:49:07.590 --> 00:49:09.490
every Monday and Tuesday night on ABC,

00:49:10.670 --> 00:49:13.003
we would think it was worth it.

00:49:14.592 --> 00:49:17.680
But yeah, you start colonizing places,

00:49:17.680 --> 00:49:19.617
you start to realize you think with,

00:49:19.617 --> 00:49:23.410
it's just not a fun,
it's not a fun colony,

00:49:23.410 --> 00:49:25.060
I'm just like, it can't be.

00:49:25.060 --> 00:49:27.388
You can bring the fun people later.

00:49:27.388 --> 00:49:28.221
At what point?

00:49:28.221 --> 00:49:31.470
But you can't have fun people
do the initial colonization.

00:49:31.470 --> 00:49:35.243
At what point do I, fun guy, get invited?

00:49:35.243 --> 00:49:36.513
I don't know if you ever get invited.

00:49:37.970 --> 00:49:41.360
I never get invited cause I'm
too tall for the space craft.

00:49:41.360 --> 00:49:43.240
6'4" is the limit.

00:49:43.240 --> 00:49:44.330
Yeah, you're too tall.

00:49:44.330 --> 00:49:45.730
So I'll never go, I'll never go.

00:49:45.730 --> 00:49:48.820
I can do maybe the antigravity flight

00:49:48.820 --> 00:49:53.054
or something like that, but
I can't, well maybe the,

00:49:53.054 --> 00:49:56.200
I might be able to take
a commercial flight,

00:49:56.200 --> 00:49:58.400
maybe they'll have some
option at some point.

00:49:58.400 --> 00:50:01.210
But another thing I wanna
talk about that hit me

00:50:01.210 --> 00:50:03.870
when we were, especially in the space

00:50:03.870 --> 00:50:05.083
vehicle mock up center.

00:50:08.530 --> 00:50:11.110
I was like, now I am not
a conspiracy theorist,

00:50:11.110 --> 00:50:14.190
I know I play one on Good
Mythical Morning from time to time

00:50:14.190 --> 00:50:17.740
but I cam not a conspiracy
theorist in actuality,

00:50:17.740 --> 00:50:20.070
and I do not believe that
there's compelling reasons

00:50:20.070 --> 00:50:22.023
to doubt that we have been to the moon.

00:50:24.010 --> 00:50:29.010
But I would say is if I
were a conspiracy theorist,

00:50:29.720 --> 00:50:33.810
specifically one who doubted
that the moon landing was real,

00:50:33.810 --> 00:50:35.960
I do not believe that
taking a tour of NASA

00:50:35.960 --> 00:50:37.260
would make me feel better.

00:50:38.700 --> 00:50:41.000
And here's what I would say,

00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:45.420
going into the space
vehicle mock up center

00:50:45.420 --> 00:50:47.270
and seeing like the red neck centaur,

00:50:48.440 --> 00:50:50.280
you're like what is happening here,

00:50:50.280 --> 00:50:52.080
like I know that they're
just experimenting with

00:50:52.080 --> 00:50:55.020
different things, but even
if, when you get inside

00:50:55.020 --> 00:50:57.890
the Lunar Module, which
we stuck our heads in,

00:50:57.890 --> 00:51:02.130
basically the kind of contraption
that's gonna go up there,

00:51:02.130 --> 00:51:06.010
you're like this all seems so tenuous,

00:51:06.010 --> 00:51:07.400
it just doesn't seem.

00:51:07.400 --> 00:51:09.060
I know it's a bunch of engineering,

00:51:09.060 --> 00:51:11.090
and it's all well thought out.

00:51:11.090 --> 00:51:12.947
But it just seems like it could just,

00:51:12.947 --> 00:51:15.990
and of course at times it
has completely fallen apart.

00:51:15.990 --> 00:51:18.140
There's so many things
that could go wrong,

00:51:19.180 --> 00:51:21.670
it just feels like I
wouldn't have a whole lot of

00:51:21.670 --> 00:51:26.570
confidence, it wasn't
the type of environment

00:51:26.570 --> 00:51:28.203
that I expected it to be.

00:51:29.070 --> 00:51:31.580
Like, the fact that we
were able to touch things,

00:51:31.580 --> 00:51:34.170
even though it was stuff that
wasn't gonna go in space,

00:51:34.170 --> 00:51:36.270
this is the mock up center,

00:51:36.270 --> 00:51:38.270
they don't let us touch the real stuff,

00:51:38.270 --> 00:51:39.760
but I just got this sense that like

00:51:39.760 --> 00:51:42.540
man if I thought this
was fake and this was all

00:51:42.540 --> 00:51:47.000
just to carry on a lie.

00:51:47.000 --> 00:51:48.500
That's quite an investment.

00:51:48.500 --> 00:51:50.250
I would probably still
believe it having seen this.

00:51:50.250 --> 00:51:51.630
The only way you could prove it to me

00:51:51.630 --> 00:51:53.910
if I was a flat Earther,
or if I didn't believe

00:51:53.910 --> 00:51:57.913
in the moon landing, I'd have
to go on the ride, you know?

00:51:57.913 --> 00:51:59.053
Have to go up.

00:51:59.970 --> 00:52:04.240
I think it's quite an investment to pull

00:52:04.240 --> 00:52:07.920
the wool over the eyes of the public.

00:52:07.920 --> 00:52:08.990
To what end?

00:52:08.990 --> 00:52:10.840
Well yeah, that's why conspiracy theories,

00:52:10.840 --> 00:52:12.130
for the most part, don't make sense,

00:52:12.130 --> 00:52:16.800
because when fully analyzed,
almost all conspiracy theories

00:52:18.370 --> 00:52:19.900
carrying out the conspiracy theory

00:52:19.900 --> 00:52:21.370
becomes more of a conspiracy

00:52:21.370 --> 00:52:23.880
than the conspiracy theory itself.

00:52:23.880 --> 00:52:25.610
And that's why most conspiracy theories,

00:52:25.610 --> 00:52:27.560
logically fall apart.

00:52:27.560 --> 00:52:31.410
But most people who want to
believe conspiracy theories

00:52:31.410 --> 00:52:34.570
cannot be swayed by logic or facts.

00:52:34.570 --> 00:52:37.830
I think, the thing that I reacted to

00:52:37.830 --> 00:52:41.310
was that a lot of it, especially
the experimental stuff,

00:52:41.310 --> 00:52:44.940
by design, was like hey, we're just gonna

00:52:44.940 --> 00:52:47.270
see where this goes, you know?

00:52:47.270 --> 00:52:51.523
This spirit of exploration, like,

00:52:52.600 --> 00:52:55.290
trudging towards a big question mark,

00:52:55.290 --> 00:52:59.790
and, you know, like
investing in the unknown.

00:52:59.790 --> 00:53:01.870
We're gonna build this thing,

00:53:01.870 --> 00:53:03.930
we're gonna have all
these college students

00:53:03.930 --> 00:53:05.730
compete to build all this
different type of stuff

00:53:05.730 --> 00:53:07.120
because they'll probably
come up with something

00:53:07.120 --> 00:53:09.790
that's awesome that we'll
need for something else

00:53:09.790 --> 00:53:11.900
and you know, they talk
about all the things,

00:53:11.900 --> 00:53:13.710
you can look at the list of all the things

00:53:13.710 --> 00:53:16.700
that we benefit from on
Earth because they were

00:53:16.700 --> 00:53:21.053
byproducts of all the
innovation for space travel.

00:53:22.590 --> 00:53:24.520
But it is infectious to be there

00:53:24.520 --> 00:53:26.483
and to see that it's just,

00:53:28.200 --> 00:53:31.208
we set, we looked up,
and then we were like

00:53:31.208 --> 00:53:35.080
let's check it out, let's bend our will,

00:53:35.080 --> 00:53:37.620
and then, you know, you talk
about people working together

00:53:37.620 --> 00:53:40.090
like on the International Space Station,

00:53:40.090 --> 00:53:41.680
you got like the whole Russian part,

00:53:41.680 --> 00:53:45.860
you got, the Japanese
built a whole rec center

00:53:45.860 --> 00:53:47.100
for the freaking space station.

00:53:47.100 --> 00:53:49.310
A gym, yeah, they had the gym module.

00:53:49.310 --> 00:53:50.770
Yeah, it's a big one.

00:53:50.770 --> 00:53:52.940
And you can, it's one of
the only places that you

00:53:52.940 --> 00:53:57.030
can send experiments out of
the International Space Station

00:53:57.030 --> 00:54:00.754
so it's not just for push ups and burpees.

00:54:00.754 --> 00:54:02.470
You're saying you could do like?

00:54:02.470 --> 00:54:03.860
They send experiments out.

00:54:03.860 --> 00:54:05.402
There's like a special hatch.

00:54:05.402 --> 00:54:07.612
You can do the elliptical
outside of the Space Station?

00:54:07.612 --> 00:54:11.180
Well, yeah, like on the Jetsons,

00:54:11.180 --> 00:54:13.340
where it's like you got
that elliptical out there.

00:54:13.340 --> 00:54:14.173
Yeah.

00:54:14.173 --> 00:54:15.006
Walk the dog on it.

00:54:16.170 --> 00:54:18.780
But, that spirit of
exploration is infectious

00:54:18.780 --> 00:54:22.379
and it was, I don't know,

00:54:22.379 --> 00:54:25.010
it made me feel good
about all the stuff we try

00:54:25.010 --> 00:54:27.470
and we don't know why we're doing it.

00:54:27.470 --> 00:54:28.490
You know?

00:54:28.490 --> 00:54:29.323
Yeah.

00:54:29.323 --> 00:54:32.710
In our own little way,
it was like a kinship.

00:54:32.710 --> 00:54:34.330
And I know that people say that a lot,

00:54:34.330 --> 00:54:37.770
they're like why worry about this

00:54:37.770 --> 00:54:40.040
when you've got, you
know, you've got people

00:54:40.040 --> 00:54:43.110
on Earth who are not
even getting properly,

00:54:43.110 --> 00:54:45.053
you know, getting proper nutrition.

00:54:47.150 --> 00:54:49.200
Well first of all I don't think that it's

00:54:51.779 --> 00:54:53.979
a net zero situation,
so I don't think that,

00:54:55.659 --> 00:54:56.570
it's not a zero sum game.

00:54:56.570 --> 00:54:58.990
So if you invest in space travel,

00:54:58.990 --> 00:55:01.520
it's necessarily means
someone's not getting a meal,

00:55:01.520 --> 00:55:03.680
but I completely agree with you that

00:55:03.680 --> 00:55:08.680
we should be trying
things just to try them.

00:55:08.870 --> 00:55:10.543
It's like why go, like,

00:55:10.543 --> 00:55:12.530
some people are like
why go to Mars, I mean,

00:55:12.530 --> 00:55:14.900
we have, we're having trouble enough

00:55:14.900 --> 00:55:19.333
keeping the inhabitable
planet, you know, habitable.

00:55:20.560 --> 00:55:22.200
Why are we gonna go someplace else that

00:55:22.200 --> 00:55:25.549
we're gonna have to geoengineer this place

00:55:25.549 --> 00:55:29.513
just in order to live there.

00:55:31.165 --> 00:55:32.460
And I think it's because
you gotta constantly

00:55:32.460 --> 00:55:34.240
be moving forward and innovating,

00:55:34.240 --> 00:55:36.380
you gotta be constantly
be trying the things.

00:55:36.380 --> 00:55:38.400
Sometimes people ask us that question,

00:55:38.400 --> 00:55:39.500
and I'm not gonna make a,

00:55:39.500 --> 00:55:40.943
I am gonna make an analogy.

00:55:41.910 --> 00:55:43.840
And I do not think that what we do

00:55:44.939 --> 00:55:46.470
in Mythical Entertainment is comparable

00:55:46.470 --> 00:55:48.530
to what is happening in NASA,

00:55:48.530 --> 00:55:51.471
even though they did have
that red neck centaur,

00:55:51.471 --> 00:55:53.190
which is the kind of thing
we've totally done on the show.

00:55:53.190 --> 00:55:54.850
We've gotten Paisley
to build one of those.

00:55:54.850 --> 00:55:55.683
Yeah.

00:55:57.160 --> 00:55:57.993
But sometimes people are like,

00:55:57.993 --> 00:56:00.630
what are you, like what
are you trying to do,

00:56:00.630 --> 00:56:02.513
what do you hope to do,

00:56:02.513 --> 00:56:04.370
like why are you trying
to keep growing this,

00:56:04.370 --> 00:56:07.040
and to me, apart of it just--

00:56:07.040 --> 00:56:08.530
The thrill of discovery.

00:56:08.530 --> 00:56:10.160
Yeah, exploration, it's just like--

00:56:10.160 --> 00:56:12.340
The thrill of expression, and discovery.

00:56:12.340 --> 00:56:15.460
Why am I gonna throw this, you know,

00:56:15.460 --> 00:56:19.010
sodium into the middle of a lake?

00:56:19.010 --> 00:56:20.330
Well we're not gonna do that.

00:56:20.330 --> 00:56:21.840
No, but there's like, you know,

00:56:21.840 --> 00:56:24.910
there are like high
school chemistry teachers

00:56:24.910 --> 00:56:29.910
who will take like pure
sodium and throw it into

00:56:30.270 --> 00:56:33.440
the middle of a lake to watch it explode.

00:56:33.440 --> 00:56:34.480
I mean you can't do that anymore,

00:56:34.480 --> 00:56:36.790
but you could probably do it in the 80s.

00:56:36.790 --> 00:56:37.700
Why do that?

00:56:37.700 --> 00:56:39.100
Because it's like well,

00:56:39.100 --> 00:56:43.114
it'd be cool to see that lake explode.

00:56:43.114 --> 00:56:47.870
I'm just saying, sometimes
you just, you start a process

00:56:47.870 --> 00:56:49.733
just to see where it's gonna end.

00:56:51.220 --> 00:56:53.950
I'm all in on space travel.

00:56:53.950 --> 00:56:54.923
I'm all in.

00:56:56.360 --> 00:56:57.193
You gonna go?

00:56:58.150 --> 00:56:59.920
I ain't gonna go unless I have to.

00:56:59.920 --> 00:57:03.280
Yeah, I'm all in into going
to the moon, going to Mars,

00:57:03.280 --> 00:57:06.100
and I don't think I
have to have like a why

00:57:06.100 --> 00:57:09.220
beyond just because let's see
if we can do it as people,

00:57:09.220 --> 00:57:11.550
let's see if we can do
it as a civilization.

00:57:11.550 --> 00:57:13.560
Yeah, well.

00:57:13.560 --> 00:57:15.980
So if Rhett ever goes up,

00:57:15.980 --> 00:57:18.493
we can credit that little tour we took.

00:57:19.940 --> 00:57:23.533
He's gonna be gripping
lovingly to a centaur rover

00:57:25.590 --> 00:57:26.780
cause I'm not gonna be there.

00:57:26.780 --> 00:57:28.633
